  "query_preview": "..\n\nBreezy southwesterly afternoon winds continue through the first half \nof the evening before diurnal mixing wanes with sunset. High \npressure centered over the Appalachians reestablishes itself over SE \nMI by Friday morning supporting much lighter winds for the day. The \nweak cold front currently over the western Great Lakes only glances \nthe region overnight as redeveloping southerly winds tied to the \naforementioned high leads to minimal change to the resident airmass. \nAs such, much above normal temperatures remain Friday as highs reach \nthe lower to mid 60s. While the influence of high pressure wanes \nSaturday in response to low pressure lifting through the upper \nMidwest, southerly flow holds across lower MI keeping temperatures \nfirmly in the 60s. Said southerly wind turns breezier daytime \nSaturday as a tightening local gradient pushes wind gusts towards 20-\n25mph. \n\nNext chances for rain arrive latter part of the day Sunday as \nnorthern Ontario low pressure slowly drags a cold front across the \nGreat Lakes. Main impact from this front however is a stark change \nin airmass to start the new week. An arctic airmass sinks southeast \nacross Ontario with SE MI residing under its southern edge. While \nthe core of this airmass (850mb temps -15C) stays to our north and \neast, local 850mb temps still fall towards -10C by Monday night. For \ndaytime Monday, ongoing northwesterly cold advection largely offsets \ndiurnal heating keeping temperatures near steady in the upper 30s to \nnear 40 though wind chills hold in the low 30s. An arctic high \npressure settles directly over the central Great Lakes by Tuesday \nturning winds lighter though maintaining below normal temperatures. \nHighs Tuesday into the midweek period only peak in the mid to upper \n30s.",
